From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hayaksi1 <193020925+hayaksi1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 3 Sep 2026 23:10:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Restore what the composer rename dropped and refresh the fork's snapshots

Upstream renamed SendMessageComposer's suite out of test/unit-tests into src/,
and three contribution branches still edit the old path. Only one of them can
win that merge, so the other two contributed nothing: pr/slash-command-double-send's
guard against a slash command running twice on a double Enter, and
pr/full-size-threads' fullSizeThreadViewEnabled fixture field. That field is a
required member of IRoomState, so losing it is a type error rather than a
failing test, and exactly three fixtures have to carry it.

Neither belongs on its branch. Each branch is correct against the path it was
written for, and both are heads of open pull requests that must not be
rewritten; the loss exists only in the combination.

The snapshots move for an unrelated reason. They embed the CSS-module hash of
stylesheets the fork edits, so every fork resolution touching EventTileView
moves the hash and every snapshot quoting it goes stale. Two earlier patches
pinned that hash by hand and broke the moment upstream moved the same file
again; regenerating from the rebuilt tree replaces both.

The hash has to be read from the built shared-components package, not from a
run against whatever dist happened to be lying around: a regeneration against
a stale build produces a patch that asserts the older hash and fails with
expected and received the wrong way round, which reads as a broken snapshot
rather than a stale build. All four files here agree on one value, and that
agreement is the check worth repeating.

The Preferences snapshot is the one worth reading rather than trusting. It is
the only place the fork's four settings rows are asserted, and a regeneration
would happily bake their absence in. All four survived: smooth scrolling, the
in-room search shortcut, its find-on-page help text, and the full-size thread
toggle.
